# Restart the app after every change

After changing anything under `Sources/`, rebuild the app bundle and restart the
running app. Do not leave the user looking at a stale build. The app does not
hot-reload, so an unrestarted app still shows the previous code.

Run this from the repository root:

```bash
osascript -e 'quit app "Factory Log"' 2>/dev/null; sleep 1
pkill -f 'MacOS/FactoryLog' 2>/dev/null; sleep 1
zsh Scripts/build-app.zsh debug
open ".build/Factory Log.app"
```

Always restart the `.app` bundle, not the bare `.build/debug/FactoryLogApp`
binary. Only the bundle has `Info.plist` and a code signature, so only it
behaves like a real GUI app.

Then confirm the new process is alive:

```bash
pgrep -fl 'MacOS/FactoryLog'
```

## Verifying the window

`swift build` succeeding is not proof the UI works. To check the app really has
an on-screen window, ask the window server. `System Events` via `osascript`
fails here with "not allowed assistive access", so do not rely on it.

`screencapture` does not work from the agent shell: it lacks Screen Recording
permission, so full-screen captures show only the wallpaper and
`screencapture -l <id>` fails with "could not create image from window". This
is not the app being broken.

To see the window, ask the debug build to draw itself. It listens for a
distributed notification whose object is `screen|output-path`. `screen` is
`today`, `yesterday`, `week` or `insights`, optionally followed by
`@<project path>` to open that project's panel in Insights. Leave it empty to
keep the current screen.

```bash
swift -e 'import Foundation; DistributedNotificationCenter.default().postNotificationName(.init("dev.factorylog.debug.snapshot"), object: "week|/tmp/week.png", userInfo: nil, deliverImmediately: true)'
```

The PNG appears about a second later. A window launched from a script draws as
inactive, so for publishable images launch with `--args -DebugActiveWindow YES`,
or run `zsh Scripts/screenshots.zsh`, which does that against a demo log. The hook only exists in debug builds,
and `Scripts/verify-release.zsh` replaces the bundle with a release build, so
rebuild with `build-app.zsh debug` before taking snapshots.

If a restored window is mispositioned or invisible, clear the saved window
state and relaunch. Don't delete the `dev.factorylog.app` defaults domain: it
holds user preferences such as hidden projects.

```bash
rm -rf ~/Library/Saved\ Application\ State/dev.factorylog.app.savedState
```
